WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.730
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about EBS volumes</v>

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.140
and EC2 instance store.

3
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.840
So first, EBS volumes are network drives.

4
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.810
And you attach them to one instance at a time,

5
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.880
except with a EBS multi-attached feature

6
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.070
that I will show you very, very soon.

7
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.350
So they're linked to a specific AZ,

8
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.810
and if you wanted to transfer them between a different AZ,

9
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.640
you need to first snapshot them,

10
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.160
and then restore them into that other AZ.

11
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.260
Your volumes can be resized, but only up,

12
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.790
so you can increase the volume size

13
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.220
without detaching the EBS volume first.

14
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.650
You need to make sure also to leverage the EBS volume

15
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.930
as much as you can to choose an EC2 instance type

16
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.030
that is going to be EBS optimized

17
00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.520
to enjoy maximum throughputs.

18
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.170
So in a diagram here, what it looks like,

19
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.670
so we have one EC2 instance,

20
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.790
it may have an EBS volume attached to it of 10 gigabytes

21
00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.410
and another EC2 instance

22
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.250
that will have maybe two EBS volumes attached to it.

23
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.470
Now for volume types, you have different types of volumes.

24
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:57.120
For EBS, you have the gp2, gp3 family, and g means general.

25
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.190
So general purpose SSD volumes

26
00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.920
that will balance a good price and performance

27
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.690
and fits most workloads.

28
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.240
But if you want extremely high performance,

29
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.310
very high throughput, very low latency,

30
00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.260
then you need to look into the i family,

31
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.680
so the io1 and io2 block express.

32
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.040
If you have a need for a low-cost HDD

33
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.900
that is going to be designed for frequently accessed

34
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.900
and throughput-intensive workload, usually sequential reads,

35
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.880
then st1 one is going to be a great choice.

36
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.380
And if you want cold storage

37
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.630
for less frequently accessed workloads,

38
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.810
then sc1 one is going to be a good use case for you.

39
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.210
The EBS volumes will have general characteristics,

40
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.580
but they will be characterized in terms of size,

41
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.990
throughputs, and IOPS, which is IO Operations Per Second.

42
00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.400
And when you're in doubt, of course,

43
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.830
always look at the AWS documentation.

44
00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.000
In terms of boot volumes,

45
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.460
which is where your operating system can be

46
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.990
for your EC2 instances,

47
00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.353
then it can only be on gp2, gp3, or io1, io2.

48
00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.360
Okay, so now snapshots.

49
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.390
So they're very helpful to transfer EBS volumes between AZ.

50
00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.130
And they're incremental,

51
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.390
so you only backup the changed blocks behind the scene.

52
00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.490
Then the backups will use IO.

53
00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.260
And so that means that because the volume's going

54
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.970
to be read by AWS,

55
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.470
if you are running an application

56
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.660
that is also handling a lot of read traffic,

57
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.520
and you are on top of it doing a backup,

58
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.730
then you may experience decreased performance.

59
00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.400
The snapshots are going to be stored in S3 in the backend,

60
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.670
but you're not going to be getting direct access to them.

61
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.280
And it's not necessary for you to detach a volume

62
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.260
to do a snapshot, but it's recommended

63
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.170
because if your volume is being actively used

64
00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.460
while you do a snapshot, you may get an inconsistent state.

65
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.000
You can copy snapshots across AZ, yes, for sure

66
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.910
and again, restore snapshots, sorry, across AZ of course,

67
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.490
but you can also copy snapshots across region

68
00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.110
for disaster recovery purposes.

69
00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.200
So it's a really good practice to snapshot, for example,

70
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.030
data in one region

71
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.070
and then copy it to another region in case you wanted

72
00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.460
to restore the data for disaster recovery purposes.

73
00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.110
Now it's also possible for you

74
00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.140
to make Amazon machine images from your snapshots

75
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.120
using the AMI feature.

76
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.470
And this is very common, for example,

77
00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.210
if you bootstrap an application

78
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.310
and then make an AMI out of it.

79
00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.520
Now when you restore an EBS volume, especially large ones,

80
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.110
then you need to prewarm them.

81
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.940
That means you need to read all the blocks

82
00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.770
to have the maximum performance.

83
00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.530
And to do so, you have two ways of doing it.

84
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.200
The first one is using a newer feature of AWS

85
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.660
because that was a very popular request.

86
00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.770
It's called the Fast Snapshot Restore, which is FSR feature.

87
00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.180
And the idea is that this feature is going

88
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.770
to read your EBS snapshot entirely and then give it to you.

89
00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.230
This way, when you restore it, it's going to be prewarmed.

90
00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.000
But if you wanted to do it the old way,

91
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.970
then you need to just attach this restored EBS volume

92
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.700
to your EC2 instance, and then you would issue the fio

93
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.080
or dd command directly from within the EC2 instance

94
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.540
to read the entire volume.

95
00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.700
Next, there is a service within EBS

96
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.430
called Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager,

97
00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.380
which is used to automate the creation,

98
00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.280
retention, and deletion of EBS snapshots and EBS-backed AMI.

99
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.560
So it's a way for you to schedule backups,

100
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.900
do cross account snapshot copies automatically,

101
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.843
or to delete outdated backups using a policy.

102
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.020
Use resource tags to identify the resources you want

103
00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.140
to back up, for example, EC2 instances or EBS volumes.

104
00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.130
And so here is an example.

105
00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.140
We can tag an EBS volume

106
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.790
with environment prod to make sure

107
00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.170
that it automatically gets backed up

108
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.360
by Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager,

109
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.430
or we can tag directly an EC2 instance

110
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.020
with this environment prod,

111
00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.693
and then the instance itself will be,

112
00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:32.460
and its EBS volumes will be backed up.

113
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:33.870
So it goes into Data Lifecycle Manager,

114
00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:37.140
and we can create snapshots and AMIs out of it.

115
00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.970
It cannot be used to manage snapshots

116
00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.530
or AMIs created outside of DLM.

117
00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:41.940
So everything has to be contained

118
00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.680
within Data Lifecycle Manager,

119
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.460
and it cannot be used to manage instance-store backed AMIs.

120
00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.410
So a question you may have is what is the difference

121
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.430
between Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager and AWS backup?

122
00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.590
So you use Data Lifecycle Manager when you want

123
00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.390
to automate the creation, retention,

124
00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.390
and deletion of EBS snapshots,

125
00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.030
whereas you're going to use AWS backups

126
00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.530
to manage and monitor backups

127
00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.140
across all the AWS services you use,

128
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.990
including EBS volumes, from a single place.

129
00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:14.220
So it's about scope, and it's about ease of management.

130
00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.340
Talking about EBS volumes, we have also EBS encryption.

131
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.820
So there is an account level setting to encrypt your volumes

132
00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.210
because by default, new EBS volumes are not encrypted.

133
00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.070
But if you turn on this setting,

134
00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.350
then automatically new EBS volumes

135
00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.690
and snapshots are going to be encrypted.

136
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.400
So in the UI, it looks like this.

137
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.400
There is a EC2 dashboard setting, and when you set it,

138
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.400
you can always encrypt new EBS volumes.

139
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.050
Just so you know, this setting,

140
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.670
even though it's an account level setting,

141
00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:45.003
needs to be enabled on the per-region basis.

142
00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:47.940
Now there's a feature called EBS multi-attach,

143
00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.180
and it's only available for the io1 and io2 family,

144
00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.560
which goes against what I told you,

145
00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.010
but it gives you the ability to attach the same EBS volumes

146
00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.040
to multiple EC2 instances in the same AZ.

147
00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.690
And each instance will have full read

148
00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.580
and write permissions on the volume.

149
00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.250
So it's only for a specific kind of applications.

150
00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.510
For example, the ones

151
00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.020
that need higher application availability, for example,

152
00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.710
Teradata that has a native support for this feature

153
00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.070
or applications that must manage concurrent write operations

154
00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.800
for this very specific use case as well.

155
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.780
Now the file system is a little bit different.

156
00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.350
It's not going to be XFS or EX4 and so on.

157
00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.120
It's going to be a file system that's cluster aware,

158
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.270
and it's not the kind of things you deal with every day.

159
00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.730
So it's a very unique feature for very unique use cases,

160
00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.140
but you need to know that it exists.

161
00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.030
And so the architecture looks like, well,

162
00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.100
you have one, for example, io2 volume

163
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.810
with a multi-attach feature,

164
00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:43.770
and it can be attached to multiple EC2 instances at a time.

165
00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:47.220
Now when you consider your local EC2 instance store,

166
00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:48.870
this one is not a network disc,

167
00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.390
it is a physical disc that is going to be attached

168
00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.270
to the physical server where your EC2 is.

169
00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.103
And while we do this,

170
00:06:55.103 --> 00:06:57.150
well, you get very, very high IOPS

171
00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:02.150
because well, the disc is attached to your EC2 instance.

172
00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.930
Now what you get on the right-hand side,

173
00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:08.007
for example, for the i3 family is a lot of write IOPS

174
00:07:08.007 --> 00:07:10.860
and a lot of read IOPS as you can see.

175
00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.290
Now the disc can vary in size, and this is just indicative,

176
00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.540
it can obviously increase over time,

177
00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.290
but the disc can go up to, for example, 7.5 terabytes.

178
00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.960
And if you strip them, so if you have them in parallel,

179
00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:24.420
you can reach, for example, 60 terabytes.

180
00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.160
And so you get a lot of IOPS.

181
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.350
It is still a block storage just like EBS, okay?

182
00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:31.110
But it is a physical device, not a network device.

183
00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.000
And it cannot be increased in size.

184
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.690
And in case your EC2 instance fails or gets shut down,

185
00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.550
you will lose your data

186
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.640
because it is a physical disc attached to your EC2 instance.

187
00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.150
So if you compare EC2 instance store and EBS volumes,

188
00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.250
while the instance store is physically attached

189
00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.920
to the machine, it is considered ephemeral storage,

190
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.660
whereas the EBS is a network drive,

191
00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.780
which is considered a persistent kind of storage.

192
00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.390
Now the pros of using the EC2 instance store is

193
00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.810
that you get a lot better IO performance.

194
00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.120
So the EBS gp2, for example,

195
00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.293
will have a max IOPS of 16,000,

196
00:08:05.293 --> 00:08:06.810
io1, 64,000,

197
00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.870
and io2 Block Express, 256,000.

198
00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.370
But we saw that the instance store

199
00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.380
can have IOPS in the millions.

200
00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:16.890
Now it's going to be very good if you want to have a buffer,

201
00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.860
a cache, scratch data, or temporary content.

202
00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.660
Remember it is ephemeral storage.

203
00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.910
And in case you reboot your EC2 instance,

204
00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.290
your data will survive.

205
00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.420
But if you happen to stop

206
00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.430
or terminate your EC2 instance,

207
00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:32.010
then all the data on the EC2 instance store

208
00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.420
is going to be lost.

209
00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:34.980
And that means that you need to manage the data.

210
00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.220
So you need to back up the data automatically to make sure

211
00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.400
that if you want to keep it, you have a mechanism to do so.

212
00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.530
And because it is an EC2 instance store,

213
00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.900
and it's a physical storage, you cannot resize it.

214
00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.810
Whereas for EBS volumes, of course you could resize it.

215
00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.370
Okay, so that's it for this lecture.

216
00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:53.370
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

